# Mastering TypeScript: Advanced Patterns

TypeScript has become the standard for large-scale JavaScript applications. This article covers advanced TypeScript patterns that will make your code more robust and maintainable.

## Discriminated Unions

Use discriminated unions to model complex state:

```typescript
type Result<T, E> =
  | { success: true; data: T }
  | { success: false; error: E };

function handleResult(result: Result<string, Error>) {
  if (result.success) {
    console.log(result.data.toUpperCase());
  } else {
    console.error(result.error.message);
  }
}
```

## Conditional Types

Create types that depend on other types:

```typescript
type NonNullable<T> = T extends null | undefined ? never : T;
type ArrayElement<T> = T extends (infer U)[] ? U : never;
```

## Template Literal Types

Build types from string patterns:

```typescript
type EventName = `on${Capitalize<string>}`;
type HTTPMethod = 'GET' | 'POST' | 'PUT' | 'DELETE';
type Endpoint = `/${string}`;
```

## Branded Types

Add semantic meaning to primitive types:

```typescript
type UserId = string & { readonly __brand: 'UserId' };
type PostId = string & { readonly __brand: 'PostId' };

function getUser(id: UserId) { /* ... */ }

const userId = 'user-123' as UserId;
getUser(userId); // OK
```

## Utility Types

Leverage built-in utility types:

- `Partial<T>` - make all properties optional
- `Required<T>` - make all properties required
- `Readonly<T>` - make all properties readonly
- `Pick<T, K>` - select specific properties
- `Omit<T, K>` - exclude specific properties
- `Record<K, V>` - create object type

## Best Practices

1. Use `unknown` instead of `any`
2. Prefer interfaces for object shapes
3. Use type aliases for unions and primitives
4. Enable strict mode in tsconfig.json
5. Use type guards for runtime checks

## Conclusion

Advanced TypeScript patterns help you write code that's both flexible and type-safe. Start incorporating these patterns gradually, and you'll see significant improvements in code quality.
